		{
			"ID": "0ac35f09-fa74-49ee-977a-3b2fcb1b3b50",
			"Type": "flow"
		}
	],
	"Description": "Single routing point for every Okta detection in this kit.\n\nEvery detection scheduled search in the kit is a dispatcher on this one alert, and\neverything that should happen when a detection fires is wired up once here as a\nconsumer. Adding a detection means adding its scheduled search as a dispatcher; it\ndoes not mean adding another alert.\n\nDispatchers\n  One scheduled search per detection, named \"ScheduledSearch - Okta - ...\". The\n  scheduled search name is the alert name: nothing inside the queries sets an\n  alert title, so what you see in the alert feed is the dispatcher that produced\n  it. Rename a scheduled search and you rename its alerts.\n\n  All dispatchers ship disabled. Enable the ones you want, a few at a time, and\n  tune before moving on.\n\nConsumers\n  One flow, \"Flow - Okta - Alert Router - Email Notification\", which emails the\n  alert to $OKTA_ALERT_RECIPIENT from $OKTA_ALERT_SENDER with links back to\n  $GRAVWELL_INSTANCE. Set those three configuration macros before enabling it, and\n  configure Account \u003e Email Server \u003e Email Server Settings.\n\n  To add a Slack, Teams, or PagerDuty destination, add a node to that one flow, or\n  add a second flow as another consumer here. Either way it applies to every\n  detection at once.\n\nFields on the events\n  Most detections ship raw Okta System Log entries with no render module, so every\n  enumerated value the query produced arrives as a field: eventType, actorAltId,\n  targetUser, clientIp, src, srcCountry, srcASNOrg, outcomeResult, outcomeReason,\n  risk_score, _priority, and the rest of the extraction.\n\n  The detections whose names end in [table] aggregate with stats instead, so they\n  ship the columns of that aggregation (counts, distinct counts, thresholds) rather\n  than raw entries. Their descriptions say which columns.\n\nTuning\n  Noise control is shared too. Add an account or an address to okta_user_allowlist\n  or okta_ip_allowlist to suppress it across every detection, or to the matching\n  denylist to force it up to priority High. See the OKTA_TUNING macro.\n\nReference(s):\n- https://developer.okta.com/docs/reference/api/event-types/\n- https://docs.gravwell.io/alerts/alerts.html",
	"Disabled": true,
